course: dictionary
lesson: 11908
title: Buoyancy: why things float
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Buoyancy is the upward push that water, or air, gives to an object. It is the reason things float or feel lighter when you are in the water. «The empty plastic bottle has plenty of buoyancy, so it bobs on the surface.»
explain: An object floats when the upward push of the water is as big as its weight. If the push is smaller than the weight, the object sinks. A stone sinks because its weight is bigger than the push the water gives it.
explain: Buoyancy has other meanings too. It can describe how well something floats: «Cork has great buoyancy.» It can also mean cheerfulness and the ability to bounce back from bad times: «Her buoyancy helped the whole team recover after the loss.»
fact: Buoyancy is a noun, and it is uncountable: you say some buoyancy, not a buoyancy.
fact: «Buoyancy» means the upward push that water or air gives to an object.
fact: Salty water gives more buoyancy than fresh water, so floating in the sea is easier than floating in a pool.
fact: Swimmers use buoyancy aids, such as armbands and floats, to help them stay up in the water.
fact: Related words: upthrust, float, sink and density.
exercise: «The big ship floats because the water pushes up on it with enough buoyancy.» What is buoyancy here? | A deep hole in the sea floor | The weight of the water itself | A kind of fish | *The upward push that water gives to an object
exercise: Why does a stone sink? | *Its weight is bigger than the upward push of the water | It is made of cork | The water is too warm | It wants to rest on the bottom
exercise: Which water gives more buoyancy? | *Salty sea water | Fresh water from a river | Cold fresh water in a pool
test: In «Her buoyancy helped the team bounce back after the loss», what does buoyancy mean? | A loud shout of anger | Water running down a hill | Heavy weight on the shoulders | *Cheerfulness and quick recovery from bad times
test: Which of these is a buoyancy aid? | *Armbands | A brick | A heavy anchor | A stone
exam: Which of these is a related word for buoyancy in science? | *Upthrust | Whistle | Table | Sunshine
